## Source review

- Read the pinned `MessageDecoder.decode` client branch and
  `MessageDecoder.decodeMessage(MessageExt, List)`. Client decode with
  `isClient=true` creates `MessageClientExt`; batch decoding creates one
  `MessageClientExt` per inner message. Pinned client
  `PullAPIWrapper.processPullResult` calls that batch decoder.
- Searched pinned Java source for classes extending `MessageExt` and for
  client construction sites. The built-in hierarchy consists of
  `MessageClientExt`, broker-side `MessageExtBrokerInner`, and
  `MessageExtBatch` (which extends `MessageExtBrokerInner`). Client source
  does not construct the broker-side types.
- Rust's retained `MessageClientExt` marker covers the standard client decode,
  pull-result, admin-decode and direct-consume paths. Existing direct-consume
  Java/Rust comparison checks listener class, effective/offset IDs and offset
  isolation.
- Arbitrary application-defined Java subclasses can still pass through
  methods typed as `MessageExt`. Rust has no dynamic subclass method/field
  dispatch for those extensions; this remains an explicit P18-V01 adaptation
  boundary.

Updated `PHASE_18_RUNTIME_CONSUMER_OPERATIONS.md` and the appended release
checklist record with the narrowed source disposition. This documentation-only
review passed `git diff --check`; no tests were run for this source inventory.
Broad P18-V01 and P18-V04 remain open.
